# Module-wide options, all LANTERN_*.

menu "Lantern"

config LANTERN_KEYS
    bool "Paint the key LEDs from the palette, the strip and the keys from the lantern,keys node"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_KEYS_ENABLED
    select LED_STRIP
    select ZMK_LOW_PRIORITY_WORK_QUEUE
    help
      On a device with a lantern,keys node (a split half of the Cyboard
      Imprint in canon): the module is the one writer of the node's strip
      (src/keys.c). Each LED shows the picture the choice LANTERN_PICTURE
      picks, by default the bands: the palette slot the node assigns to the
      key under it (positions: the key under each LED, in chain order;
      slots: the slot of each key position), so the palette's colours fall
      on the keyboard in bands. A slot that is 0x000000 shows slot 0, and a
      device without a palette shows white (LANTERN_NO_PICTURE's black
      aside). A frame is written when the LEDs
      light or go dark (LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER) and when the palette changes,
      on ZMK's low priority work queue, which this selects.

      CONFIG_ZMK_RGB_UNDERGLOW must be n on such a device (asserted): its
      tick would repaint the strip. The strip's chain-length must be the LED
      count, one positions entry each, and slots has one entry per key
      position of the keymap (both asserted). Default y where the node is
      present: the node is the switch, and a target without one (a dongle)
      builds none of the painting.

config LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS
    int "Brightness of the key LEDs, in percent of full scale: the picture's unless LANTERN_PICTURE_BRIGHTNESS is lower, and the base of the frame's cap"
    range 1 100
    default 16
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    help
      Every palette colour is scaled so that its brightest channel is a
      percentage of full scale (255), which keeps its hue and saturation, a
      grey or black scaling to white: this one, unless
      LANTERN_PICTURE_BRIGHTNESS sets the picture lower. Whatever the
      picture, a frame draws at most every LED white at this percentage plus
      two keys white at full scale (src/keys.c). 16 is what ZMK's underglow
      drove on canon's halves before this module painted the strip
      (brightness 25 between Cyboard's 5 and 50).

config LANTERN_PICTURE_BRIGHTNESS
    int "Brightness of the picture alone, in percent of full scale, at most LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS"
    range 1 LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS
    default LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    help
      The picture's colours, the countdown's other pictures and the stain's
      dye at this percentage of full scale instead of LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS, so
      that the picture can be dimmer than the effects over it: an effect
      still brightens to full scale and a frame's cap stays the one
      LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS sets, so a dimmer picture leaves the effects as much
      room or more. Colours that paint alike at this brightness count once,
      so a dimmer picture can merge two close colours: one picture fewer in
      the countdown and one colour fewer in an effect's cycle. Default
      LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS; never above it (asserted: a still picture could
      pass the cap). canon t-skdt tries half of it under the disc.

config LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    bool "Light the key LEDs while USB powers this half, dark and unpowered on battery"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_RAIL_ENABLED
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    depends on GPIO
    help
      On a device with a lantern,rail node (a split half of the Cyboard
      Imprint in canon): while USB powers the device, the LED rail is on and
      the key LEDs show the palette's picture (LANTERN_KEYS); on battery the
      LEDs are black and the rail is off, so they draw nothing from the
      battery (src/on_usb_power.c). The rail is the node's gpios, the pin
      ZMK's zmk,ext-power-generic node would drive as control-gpios; that
      node stays disabled in the consumer's overlay (the source says why),
      and a gpio hog may hold the pin low from reset until the module takes
      it.

      Needs CONFIG_USB_DEVICE_STACK (ZMK's default on nRF52840, the split
      peripheral included) and CONFIG_SETTINGS; the source asserts each at
      build time, so a wrong conf fails the build with a message. Default y
      where the node is present: the node is the switch, and a target without
      one (a dongle) builds none of the module.

config LANTERN_BOOT_LIGHT_MS
    int "Light the key LEDs at boot for this many milliseconds (0 = no boot light)"
    range 0 60000
    default 1000
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    depends on !LANTERN_INTRO
    help
      At boot the rail goes on and the key LEDs show the palette's picture
      for this long from its frame, on USB and on battery alike; then
      LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER's rule applies: on USB the LEDs stay lit, on
      battery they go black and the rail off. 0: no boot light, the rule
      applies at once. LANTERN_NO_PICTURE, which paints every key black,
      shows the bands for it, so that a half still shows it has power.
      With LANTERN_INTRO the boot light plays the intro for the intro's own
      length, and this option is gone (set anyway, Kconfig only warns and
      drops it).

config LANTERN_INTRO
    bool "Play the intro, red, blue and green from the outer keys to the thumbs, at boot and when USB power arrives"
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    select LANTERN_FRAMES
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The boot light plays the intro, a six-second show (src/keys.c tells
      it), instead of the picture, and opens at boot, cable or not, and
      again whenever USB power arrives, so a cable plugged in shows it too,
      into an idle half as well; then LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER's rule applies,
      as after any boot light. Nothing cuts it short, and power that arrives
      while it plays does not restart it. Each half plays it for itself,
      mirrored, from its outermost key to its innermost thumb, measured on
      the lantern,keys node's coords (asserted). Its colours are its own, not
      the palette's: red, blue and green up to full scale under the frame's
      cap, and no other but the star's white head, tinted where it meets the
      glitter (canon t-5v1f: the user's pick, after Pokemon's first games). A
      key pressed while it plays shows no effect.

config LANTERN_OFF_WHEN_IDLE
    bool "Dark once ZMK's activity state leaves ACTIVE, lit again on activity"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    help
      While USB powers the device, the LEDs go dark and the rail off, as on
      battery, once ZMK turns IDLE: CONFIG_ZMK_IDLE_TIMEOUT ms (ZMK's default
      30000) without a key, sensor or pointing input on this device, each
      split half for itself. The next input lights them again, settle-ms
      after the rail. The boot light is not cut short. The timeout is the
      consumer's line (canon's halves set 300000); a cable plugged into an
      idle device lights nothing until its next input (the source says why),
      but with LANTERN_INTRO it plays the intro and goes dark at its end.

config LANTERN_COUNTDOWN
    bool "Count down to the idle dark in the palette's colours, a stage a colour"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_OFF_WHEN_IDLE
    depends on LANTERN_PALETTE
    help
      While the key LEDs are lit, each half counts the time since its last key
      in stages of LANTERN_COUNTDOWN_STAGE_MS and changes the picture at each:
      the bands, then the whole half in each colour of the palette in turn (a
      cleared slot is slot 0's colour; colours that paint alike at
      LANTERN_PICTURE_BRIGHTNESS count once), then the bands again, until ZMK
      turns idle and LANTERN_OFF_WHEN_IDLE blanks the LEDs. A key brings the
      bands back and restarts the clock; a cable plugged in lights the stage
      the keys say (src/keys.c). A sensor or pointing input counts for ZMK's
      idle and not for this clock. One colour: the bands until the dark.

config LANTERN_COUNTDOWN_STAGE_MS
    int "Milliseconds between two pictures of the countdown"
    range 100 3600000
    default 10000
    depends on LANTERN_COUNTDOWN
    help
      How long each picture of the countdown shows before the next: the
      bands, then each colour of the palette, then the bands again, until
      ZMK's idle blanks the LEDs. Ten seconds is what canon's user chose on
      hardware (2026-10-20, t-x82q); a minute a colour, with the colours
      running out at the idle timeout, read as still.

choice LANTERN_PICTURE
    prompt "The key LEDs' picture, under the countdown and the effects"
    default LANTERN_BANDS
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    help
      What the LEDs show while lit, the countdown's first picture and the
      one the effects draw over (src/keys.c), or every key black
      (LANTERN_NO_PICTURE). The bands by default; canon t-skdt tries the
      others one image at a time.

config LANTERN_BANDS
    bool "Each key in the slot the lantern,keys node's slots give it"
    help
      Each LED shows the palette slot the node's slots table gives the key
      under it, so the palette's colours fall on the keyboard in the bands
      the consumer draws (canon: five, slots 0..4 left to right).

config LANTERN_SOLID
    bool "Every key in slot 0, the heaviest colour"
    help
      Every LED shows slot 0, the whole half in one colour. The countdown's
      pictures are then the whole half in each distinct colour in turn
      (colours that paint alike count once), slot 0's first. The node keeps
      slots, which this picture does not read (the crossing ring and the
      beams do).

config LANTERN_GRADIENT
    bool "The palette's colours blended across the board from left to right, by each key's x"
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      Each LED shows a blend of the palette's colours by its key's place on
      the whole board (the lantern,keys node's coords): the leftmost key in
      the first distinct colour, the rightmost in the last, and each key
      between them the two colours around its place mixed in proportion, in
      slot order, so no two neighbours meet at an edge. A blend's brightest
      channel is the picture's brightness (LANTERN_PICTURE_BRIGHTNESS), as a
      colour's (a blend of two hues
      lights more channels, so it can look brighter). The halves share the
      board: the left half shows the first stretch, the right half the last.
      A colour shows pure only on a key at its place; no key sits between
      the halves, so a colour whose place lies there never does. The
      countdown's other pictures are as with the bands. Needs the node's
      coords (asserted). The
      node keeps slots, which this picture does not read (the crossing ring
      and the beams do).

config LANTERN_SCATTER
    bool "Throw the palette's slots over the keys at random, by the node's weights, a new throw every LANTERN_SCATTER_MS"
    depends on ENTROPY_HAS_DRIVER || TEST_RANDOM_GENERATOR
    help
      The palette's slots thrown over the half's LEDs at random: each slot
      on as many keys as its share of the lantern,keys node's
      scatter-weights (one percentage per slot, at most 100 in all,
      asserted; slots 1.. take their rounded share, slot 0, the heaviest
      colour, the rest), thrown again every LANTERN_SCATTER_MS while the
      LEDs are lit, keys or no keys, and at every light. Each half throws
      for itself. The node keeps slots, which this picture does not read
      (the crossing ring and the beams do).
      Needs a random number source (Zephyr's sys_rand32_get: an entropy
      driver, as the nRF52840's RNG, or the test generator).

config LANTERN_NO_PICTURE
    bool "No picture: every key black while lit, so that only the effects light"
    help
      Every LED black while the LEDs are lit, so that only the effects
      show, over black keys (and the stain's dye, which stays at the
      picture's brightness as over any picture). The boot light
      (LANTERN_BOOT_LIGHT_MS) shows the bands instead, so that a half still
      shows it has power, unless LANTERN_INTRO plays in its place; on USB
      the keys turn black when it ends. With the
      countdown on, its first picture is the black one and the others the
      whole half in a colour, as with the bands, from
      LANTERN_COUNTDOWN_STAGE_MS after the last key; canon's product images
      carry it with LANTERN_COUNTDOWN=n (t-skdt). The node keeps slots, which
      the boot light reads here (not with LANTERN_INTRO) and the crossing
      ring for its colours.

endchoice

config LANTERN_SCATTER_MS
    int "Milliseconds between two throws of the scatter"
    range 1000 3600000
    default 10000
    depends on LANTERN_SCATTER
    help
      How long each throw of the scatter shows before the next, while the
      LEDs are lit. Ten seconds is canon's user's first pick (t-skdt).

config LANTERN_FRAMES
    bool
    help
      The moving frames in src/keys.c: a frame every 30 ms while something
      moves, and the cap on a frame's draw. The effects' engine and the
      intro select it; nothing to set.

config LANTERN_EFFECTS
    bool
    select LANTERN_FRAMES
    help
      The effects' engine in src/keys.c: the ring of the half's last
      presses and the pull from the picture toward an effect's colour, on
      the moving frames and their cap (LANTERN_FRAMES), and the pick of
      which built effects draw (LANTERN_EFFECT, the choice
      LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT). Each effect selects it; nothing to set.

config LANTERN_EFFECT
    bool "Take the pick of the effects from the split central's host, and keep it across boots"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_BEHAVIOR_EFFECT_ENABLED
    depends on SETTINGS
    help
      Which of the effects built into a half's image draw, chosen at run
      time: the host hands the split central a pick, one or more of the
      effects by name (LANTERN_PORT), the central invokes the
      lantern,behavior-effect node, which ZMK's split transport carries to
      every half by its name (the node is named effect on every device,
      src/effect.c, the binding), and the central and every half keep the
      pick in their settings ("lantern/effect/mask"). A half draws the
      picked effects it builds; with no pick stored, or none of its effects
      built, it draws the one LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT names. On a split
      central the pick is resent to a half at each of its battery reports,
      with the palette (src/palette.c says why that signal), so a half that
      was away gets it when it is back; the run queue must then hold the
      palette's three invocations and this one for every half at once,
      CONFIG_ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_CENTRAL_SPLIT_RUN_QUEUE_SIZE at least 4 x the
      peripheral count (8 for two halves; asserted). canon's
      scripts/dongle.py effect sends the pick (t-5hqx), and changes the
      effect of the day without flashing. Default y where the node is
      present: the node is the switch, as the palette's.

choice LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT
    prompt "The effect a half draws while no pick is stored, or none of the pick's effects is built"
    depends on LANTERN_EFFECTS
    default LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_STAIN if LANTERN_STAIN
    help
      One of the effects built into the image (each member depends on its
      effect): what a half shows before the host's first pick (src/effect.c)
      and whenever the stored pick names no effect this image builds, and
      the only pick a consumer without a host has. canon's is the crossing
      ring (t-5hqx). Without a line: the stain when it is built (it builds
      the thin ring too, and a conf that asks for the stain wants the dye;
      Opus review 2026-10-26), else the first built effect in this order. A
      conf that builds several effects for a keyboard without a host writes
      the line.

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_THIN_RING
    bool "The thin ring"
    depends on LANTERN_THIN_RING

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_DISC
    bool "The disc"
    depends on LANTERN_DISC

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_STAIN
    bool "The stain, the thin ring that dyes"
    depends on LANTERN_STAIN

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_CROSS_RING
    bool "The crossing ring"
    depends on LANTERN_CROSS_RING

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_BEAMS
    bool "The beams"
    depends on LANTERN_BEAMS

config LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT_WATER
    bool "The water"
    depends on LANTERN_WATER

endchoice

config LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    bool
    help
      The board's geometry for what measures it (src/keys.c): the
      lantern,keys node's coords, an x and a y per key position in hundredths
      of a key unit (asserted present and twice the key count), and the
      distance between two keys through an integer square root. Each effect
      that measures the board selects it, the intro, and the gradient
      picture for the coords alone; nothing to set.

config LANTERN_THIN_RING
    bool "Build the thin ring: a thin ring of the palette's next colour from each pressed key"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The effect thin-ring (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn
      when the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT):
      while the key LEDs are lit, a ring one key wide spreads from each key
      this half presses (and, where the hit node exists, LANTERN_HIT, from
      the other half's keys too, in across the split) at twelve key units a
      second and fades out over a
      second, so it has crossed the half when it is gone; each press takes
      the palette's next colour, at full brightness (so the build refuses a
      LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100), and a key under two rings shows the
      stronger one's colour. Needs the lantern,keys node's coords, each key
      position's x and y in hundredths of a key unit (asserted): src/keys.c
      measures the ring on the board. Default n: a consumer builds the
      effects it wants to pick from.

config LANTERN_DISC
    bool "Build the disc: a disc from each pressed key, brightest at its rim, the palette's colours passing inside"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The effect disc (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn when
      the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT): while
      the key LEDs are lit, a rim spreads from each key this half
      presses (and, where the hit node exists, LANTERN_HIT, from the other
      half's keys too, in across the split: the disc then lives long enough
      to cross the whole board) at twelve key units a second; a key it has
      passed lights at
      full brightness (so the build refuses a LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100) and
      fades back to the picture over half a second, its
      colour running once through the palette's colours in order as it
      fades, so the disc is brightest at its rim, dark behind it, and the
      day's colours pass inside it. A key under two discs shows the stronger
      one's colour. Needs the lantern,keys node's coords, as the thin ring
      does. Default n, as the thin ring.

config LANTERN_STAIN
    bool "Build the stain: the thin ring dyeing the keys it passes in its colour, until the countdown moves the picture"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_THIN_RING
    help
      The effect stain (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn when
      the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT; the pick's
      thin-ring is the ring without the dye, and this is the ring with it):
      the thin ring (LANTERN_THIN_RING, selected) leaves its colour behind: a
      key within about 4.8 key units of the press, where the ring's crest
      still has 0.6 of its full strength (it fades as it spreads), keeps the
      ring's colour at the picture's brightness from the moment the crest
      reaches it (where two rings have passed, the newer press's colour), so
      the half is painted over in the day's colours as you
      type, each press in the palette's next colour (a ring in from the
      other half, LANTERN_HIT, dyes the keys within its reach too, the
      thumbs first). The dye goes when the
      countdown changes the picture (LANTERN_COUNTDOWN_STAGE_MS after the
      last key; the next key brings the picture back and its ring dyes it
      afresh), when the pick changes and whenever the LEDs light; without
      the countdown it stays until the LEDs go dark. Default n, as the thin
      ring.

config LANTERN_CROSS_RING
    bool "Build the crossing ring: a thin ring from each pressed key across the whole board, both halves, in the key's band colour"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    select LANTERN_HIT
    help
      The effect cross-ring (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn
      when the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT):
      while the key LEDs are lit, a ring one key wide spreads from each
      pressed key at twelve key units a second over the whole board: from a
      key of this half out through the other half, and from a key of the
      other half, which the split central relays here (LANTERN_HIT,
      selected: this half and the central need the hit node, on a BLE
      split), in at the thumbs and then the inner columns. The ring keeps
      its strength until it has left the board, a second and a half on
      canon's Imprint, where the thin ring fades within a half; its colour
      is the pressed key's band (the lantern,keys node's slots), so both
      halves draw one press in one colour, at full brightness within the
      frame's cap (so the build refuses a LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100); a key
      under two rings shows the stronger one's colour. Needs the node's
      coords, as the thin ring does. Default n, as the thin ring.

config LANTERN_BEAMS
    bool "Build the beams: from each pressed key, light running outward along its row and its column, in the key's band colour"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The effect beams (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn when
      the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT): while the
      key LEDs are lit, four beams leave each pressed key at twelve key
      units a second, two along its row and two along its column, each
      four fifths of a key wide and fading out over a second, so the row's
      have left the half well before they are gone (and, where the hit node
      exists, LANTERN_HIT, a press of the other half sends its row's beam
      in across the split: from an inner column it arrives at about half
      strength, from an outer one all but spent, from a thumb nearly at
      full strength). A key is on the row or the column when its y or x is
      within half a key unit of the pressed key's, so canon's thumb
      clusters, half a unit off the bottom row and the inner column, make
      rows and columns of their own, two rows of three and three columns of
      two, and the two clusters share their rows. The colour is the
      pressed key's band (the lantern,keys node's slots), as the crossing
      ring's, so both halves draw one press in one colour, at full
      brightness within the frame's cap (so the build refuses a
      LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100); a key under two beams shows the stronger
      one's colour. Needs the node's coords, as the thin ring does. Default
      n, as the thin ring.

config LANTERN_WATER
    bool "Build the water: from each pressed key, waves over the keys, crests in the palette's first colour and troughs in its last"
    depends on LANTERN_KEYS
    select LANTERN_EFFECTS
    select LANTERN_GEOMETRY
    help
      The effect water (src/effect.h), built into the image and drawn when
      the pick names it (LANTERN_EFFECT, LANTERN_EFFECT_DEFAULT): while the
      key LEDs are lit, each pressed key starts waves, a front spreading at
      twelve key units a second with a wavelength of two key units behind
      it, fading out over a second and a half and weaker the farther from
      the key (and, where the hit node exists, LANTERN_HIT, a press of the
      other half starts waves too, which come in across the split faint).
      The waves of every press add up: where two crests meet the surface
      rises higher, where a crest meets a trough it stays still. A key is
      pulled from the picture toward the palette's first colour on a crest
      and its last colour in a trough, the colours between on the slopes,
      and as far as the surface is from still, so the still surface shows
      the picture (black with the no picture) and a key under the waves
      flickers between the two colours six times a second; at full
      brightness within the frame's cap (so the build refuses a
      LANTERN_BRIGHTNESS of 100). zmk-advanced-underglow-effects-vfx's
      water, from its idea (nothing copied). Needs the node's coords, as
      the thin ring does. Default n, as the thin ring.

config LANTERN_HIT
    bool "Carry each half's key presses to the other half through the split central, for the effects that cross the split"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_BEHAVIOR_HIT_ENABLED
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT_BLE
    help
      The split central sends each key press, a half's or its own, to every
      other half as one invocation of the lantern,behavior-hit node, which
      ZMK's split transport carries by the node's name: the node is named
      hit on every device (src/hit.c, the binding), param1 the key position.
      A half notes a relayed press as a hit for its effects while its LEDs
      are lit, as one of its own keys: the crossing ring draws it from the
      other half's key, the beams, the thin ring, the disc and the water do
      too, in their own colours (the beams' row comes in across the split,
      the disc then lives long enough to cross the board, the water's waves
      come in faint), the
      stain dyes with it, and it counts in the press count the thin ring's
      colours follow, whichever of them the pick draws; its countdown's
      clock ignores it. The central sends
      only to a half whose link runs at peripheral latency 0, which a half
      asks for while USB powers it (LANTERN_USB_LATENCY, on by default): a
      half on battery is dark, and writes to it would wait up to 31
      connection events each and, in a run, dry the TX pool on which the run
      queue's drain blocks with the lit half's writes behind; a consumer
      that turns LANTERN_USB_LATENCY off on its halves gets no relay. The
      sending runs on a work queue of the module's own, never the system
      work queue, and the hits share the central's run queue with the
      palette (src/hit.c says why, and what a vanished half costs). Default
      y where the node is present on a BLE split: the node is the switch,
      as the palette's.

config LANTERN_USB_LATENCY
    bool "Ask the split central for peripheral latency 0 while USB powers this half"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT_BLE
    depends on !ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    help
      On a split peripheral whose key LEDs light on USB power: while USB
      powers it, the half asks the central for peripheral latency 0 on
      their link, so what the central sends it (a palette, a relayed key
      press) arrives at the next connection event, not up to 31 events
      later (232.5 ms at ZMK's 7.5 ms interval and latency 30); on battery
      it asks for the latency the link had at connection, the central's,
      so the battery pays nothing. The interval and the timeout stay the
      link's. Zephyr holds a peripheral's request for the first
      CONFIG_BT_CONN_PARAM_UPDATE_TIMEOUT ms of a connection (5 s), and the
      central's host accepts it; src/latency.c says why and how.

config LANTERN_PALETTE
    bool "Keep a palette of six colours, set from the split central's host; the key LEDs show them"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_LANTERN_BEHAVIOR_PALETTE_ENABLED
    depends on SETTINGS
    help
      Six colours (0xRRGGBB, slots 0..5) that the host hands the split
      central (LANTERN_PORT) and that the central and every half keep
      in their settings ("lantern/palette/colours"); a half's key LEDs show
      them in the picture (LANTERN_KEYS; the bands by default), repainted
      when a palette arrives while they are lit and shown at every boot. A
      set of fewer than six colours clears the rest to 0x000000, which the
      LEDs show as slot 0. The central's code invokes the
      lantern,behavior-palette node, which ZMK's split transport carries to
      every half by its name: the node is named palette on every device
      (src/palette.c, the binding), one pair of slots an invocation, three
      a palette. On a split central the palette is resent to a
      half at each of its battery reports, the first of which follows every
      (re)connection, so a half that was away gets it when it is back: that
      needs CONFIG_ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_CENTRAL_BATTERY_LEVEL_FETCHING, asserted at
      build time; and its run queue must hold a whole palette for every
      half at once, CONFIG_ZMK_SPLIT_BLE_CENTRAL_SPLIT_RUN_QUEUE_SIZE at
      least 3 x the peripheral count (ZMK's default is 5), asserted too, and
      one more a half with the pick (LANTERN_EFFECT: 8 for two halves).
      Default y where the node is present: the node is the switch, as the
      lantern,rail node is for LANTERN_ON_USB_POWER.

config LANTERN_PORT
    bool "Take palette and effect commands on the split central's USB CDC ACM port"
    default y
    depends on LANTERN_PALETTE || LANTERN_EFFECT
    depends on ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL
    depends on USB_CDC_ACM
    select UART_INTERRUPT_DRIVEN
    help
      Text lines on any CDC ACM port of the split central (src/port.c is the
      whole grammar): "palette RRGGBB [RRGGBB ...]" (one to six colours)
      sets slots 0.. on every half and on the central and clears the rest,
      answered with "palette ok" and the six slots, and "palette" or
      "palette?" asks for them (LANTERN_PALETTE); "effect <name> [<name>
      ...]" (src/effect.h's names) picks the effects on every half and on
      the central, answered with "effect ok" and the names, and "effect" or
      "effect?" asks (LANTERN_EFFECT). canon's scripts/dongle.py palette and
      effect send them to the Imprint Dongle. The port's rate is untouched:
      1200 baud is zmk-beacon's bootloader entry. A reply is written without
      waiting for a reader; a line that begins with neither word gets none.
      It takes the UART interrupt callback of every CDC ACM port, of which a
      device keeps one, which is why one file reads both words: a consumer
      that gives a port to a shell or another reader sets this n.

endmenu
